{"id": "6bd77e5a-1884-44c0-a79c-f9f74ce96309", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "This is a great place to start — most teams ask about DR before they've set RPO/RTO or picked a strategy, so let's map out where you stand today across all four failure scenarios (one service failing, an AZ failing, the whole Region failing, and bad data/accidental deletion) before we talk targets. Let me check your three resources (MySQL, Valkey, DynamoDB) plus anything connected to them — resilience setup, backups, cross-Region copies, and surrounding infrastructure — in eu-central-1.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_ZYEswGIPaNbyYt3Vo9erVn", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Gather read-only disaster-recovery posture data for a workload in eu-central-1 (AWS account 012345678901_1), following the resilience-review skill's method (Step 0 workload mapping, Step 2 data-store classification, Step 5 layer checks). Read the resilience-review skill yourself via skill_read before starting, and follow its 'If you delegate data gathering' section: return DATA ONLY, one row per resource, in the exact format it specifies (layer | resource | type | Region | in-Region redundancy | backups | copies/images/IaC outside Region | point-in-time recovery | replication | what could not be read). Do not write the final prose report - I will write it.",
  "context": "User named three resources explicitly: an Amazon RDS for MySQL instance 'dr-demo-mysql', an Amazon ElastiCache (Valkey engine) replication group 'dr-demo-valkey', and a DynamoDB table 'dr-demo-players', all in eu-central-1. They want disaster recovery for a Region failure or a single-service failure, and have not yet defined RPO or RTO. Find these exact resources (do not substitute similar-named ones - if any is missing, say so clearly). Also map anything connected to them by configuration (same CloudFormation stack, security groups, app tier that reads/writes them) to find the full workload boundary - e.g. compute (EC2/ECS/EKS/Lambda) that talks to these data stores, load balancers/API Gateway in front, networking (VPC/NAT/endpoints), secrets/KMS keys they use. First check AWS Resilience Hub (next generation, resiliencehubv2) via ListServices in eu-central-1 to see if this workload is already registered there - if so follow the skill's Resilience Hub branch instead (GetService, GetPolicy, ListFailureModeFindings) and return that data too.",
  "resources": ["dr-demo-mysql (RDS MySQL)", "dr-demo-valkey (ElastiCache Valkey)", "dr-demo-players (DynamoDB table)", "eu-central-1", "account 012345678901_1"],
  "timeframe": "current state, not time-bounded",
  "criteria": "For RDS: MultiAZ, BackupRetentionPeriod, ReadReplicaDBInstanceIdentifiers, DeletionProtection, StorageEncrypted, automated backup replication to another region, manual/automated snapshots and their regions. For ElastiCache/Valkey: number of member clusters/replicas, AutomaticFailover, MultiAZ, SnapshotRetentionLimit, GlobalReplicationGroupInfo, node type/family (for Global Datastore eligibility). For DynamoDB: DescribeContinuousBackups (PointInTimeRecoveryStatus, RecoveryPeriodInDays), ListBackups, DescribeTable Replicas (global table or not). Also check AWS Backup: ListBackupPlans, ListProtectedResources for these three resources, DescribeRegionSettings (DynamoDB advanced features on/off), and whether any copy rules target another region with actual recovery points landed there. Check for any compute/entry-point/network layer connected to these three stores (CloudFormation stacks, tags, security groups referencing them). Note explicitly which calls were denied or returned empty so I can mark them 'not checked' rather than 'no gap'."
}", "deep_research": true}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Gather read-only DR posture data for dr-demo workload in eu-central-1."}], "created_at": null}
